<h1>Alexi Giannoulias</h1>
<p>Candidate for Mayor, Chicago, on the ballot February 23, 2027</p>
<p><a href="https://alexiforus.com" rel="nofollow noopener">Campaign website</a></p>
<p><a href="https://alexiforchi.com/policy/" rel="nofollow noopener">Alexi Giannoulias&#39;s campaign lists 17 proposals under three headings: less crime, more money in your pockets, and a City Hall that works. Tap here to read them on his site, then come back to comment.</a></p>
<h2>AI summary</h2>
<h3>The platform</h3>
<p>Safety: Giannoulias would add 1,000 officers to walk the beat, bring back gunshot detection, and raise funding for community violence intervention by 25%. He would put 100 more police on CTA trains.</p>
<p>Costs: Raise the minimum wage from $17 to $22 by 2032, hold the city&#39;s property tax levy flat, and expand free full-day preschool for 3- and 4-year-olds.</p>
<p>Housing: Build 50,000 homes by 2032 by easing zoning, speeding permits, and allowing pre-approved building plans.</p>
<p>City Hall: Cut middle management and the vehicle fleet, and merge the city&#39;s four pension funds.</p>
<h3>Next to the other candidates</h3>
<p>Only Giannoulias: A $22 minimum wage by 2032, and making new data centers pay the full cost of their power.</p>
<p>Taxes: Giannoulias would hold the city&#39;s property tax levy flat and have the wealthy pay a bigger share. Holberg and Wilson promise lower taxes, and Carlisle proposes a new income tax tied to pension reform.</p>
<p>Housing target: Quigley and Giannoulias both aim for 50,000 homes.</p>
<p>Pensions: Giannoulias would merge the four city pension funds. Wilson wants a state constitutional amendment, and Carlisle ties reform to a new income tax.</p>
